 # Holistic Accuracy and the Lockean Thesis
 
-**Abstract.** Dorst argues that the Lockean thesis follows from the core of accuracy-first epistemology even when the accuracy of a belief state is not the sum of the accuracies of its members. I show that this separability-free derivation is invalid. Its steps hold exactly when accuracy is separable, so it does not reach the holist it addresses. A natural holistic measure, which adds a premium for a belief state with no false member, satisfies all of Dorst's stated premises yet violates the Lockean thesis for every positive premium: rational belief in a proposition can be lost as credence in it rises. Under this measure one believes the sufficiently probable consequences of a working hypothesis chosen for its probability and informativeness. Expected accuracy is therefore neutral between Lockean and guess-like belief. It has a limit, though: no truth-directed value for answers yields the split-invariant belief of normality theories.
+**Abstract.** Does maximizing expected accuracy force a Lockean threshold rule for belief even when accuracy is holistic? Dorst argues that it does. I identify a gap in the derivation and give a counterexample on the unrestricted space of belief states. A score that adds a premium for having no false beliefs is strictly truth-directed and makes the rational assessment of final states independent of their order of formation. Yet, for every positive premium, it permits belief in a proposition at one credence and requires withholding it at a higher credence. The optimum combines a selected core hypothesis with a probability threshold. I also characterize a pair of sufficient repairs to the derivation: together they recover separability on states without complementary pairs. This does not establish that separability is necessary for every Lockean representation. It establishes that truth-directed accuracy and order independence alone do not settle the form of the bridge from credence to belief.
 
 ## 1. Introduction
 
-Accuracy-first epistemology holds that rational doxastic states are those that best promote accuracy by the agent's own lights. Applied to outright belief, with true beliefs scored positively and false ones negatively, it yields a Lockean thesis: believe a proposition just in case your credence in it exceeds a threshold. The separable version of this result is due to Easwaran (2016; see Dorst 2019, 7). Dorst (2019) extended it in two directions. Letting the values of true and false belief vary with proposition and context yields variable thresholds (his Corollary 2). And, he argued, dropping Separability, the assumption that a belief state's accuracy is the sum of the accuracies of its members, changes nothing: given a further premise, Sequence-Irrelevance, Lockeanism follows anyway (his Proposition 5). Hence "The connection between epistemic utility theory and the Lockean Thesis is robust" (16), and "Lockeanism looked inevitable" (17).[^1]
+Accuracy-first epistemology holds that rational doxastic states are those that best promote accuracy by the agent's own lights. With a separable score for outright belief, positive for true beliefs and negative for false ones, it yields a Lockean thesis: believe a proposition just in case your credence in it exceeds a threshold. The separable version of this result is due to Easwaran (2016; see Dorst 2019, §3). Dorst (2019) extended it in two directions. Letting the values of true and false belief vary with proposition and context yields variable thresholds (his Corollary 2). And, he argued, dropping Separability, the assumption that a belief state's accuracy is the sum of the accuracies of its members, changes nothing: given a further premise, Sequence-Irrelevance, Lockeanism follows anyway (his Proposition 5). The resulting claim is that the Lockean form survives a substantial relaxation of separability.[^1]
 
-I argue that it is not robust. My thesis is that whether maximizing expected accuracy yields Lockean belief turns on whether accuracy is separable: Dorst's separability-free derivation is valid only for separable measures, and a natural holistic measure that satisfies all its stated premises yields non-Lockean belief instead. Three results support this. Theorem 1 locates two unstated premises in the derivation and shows that together they amount to Separability. Theorem 2 gives the counterexample: a measure that adds a premium for an error-free belief state meets every premise Dorst states and violates Proposition 5 for every positive premium, because it makes belief non-monotone in credence. Theorem 3 says what that measure delivers instead. Finally, Theorem 4 marks a limit: whatever holism buys, it does not buy the split-invariant belief of knowledge-aimed theories.
+The broad robustness claim needs qualification. The counterexample below preserves unrestricted choice, truth-directed value, and order-independent assessment of final states. It nevertheless violates a fixed, proposition-specific threshold rule. Theorem 2 establishes that violation for every positive value of a premium for an error-free record. Theorem 3 characterizes the resulting optima. Theorem 1 diagnoses the derivation by identifying a pair of sufficient additional assumptions that restore a separable score. It does not prove that every successful derivation of Lockeanism must use them.
 
-The thesis does not say that Lockeanism or Separability is false, that holistic accuracy yields deductively closed belief in general (it need not), or that Dorst's reduction of belief to credence fails (§5).
+The thesis does not say that Lockeanism or separability is false. Nor does it say that holistic accuracy guarantees consistency or deductive closure: the characterization below states exactly when those properties hold. The question is what follows from the advertised minimal premises.
 
-The closest prior work is Dorst's. Rothschild, after characterizing Lockean belief sets by additive scoring, writes that "Most interesting, I think, would be to see what can be found when the additivity assumption is weakened" (2021 preprint, 15). Wang (2025) obtains non-Lockean belief from expected-utility maximization. According to its abstract, which is all I have been able to read, it concludes that "deductively cogent expected accuracy maximizers are neither Lockean nor Humean." Those maximizers choose among deductively cogent belief states. Dorst's Accuracy First forbids exactly this restriction. He says that deductive closure, imposed from outside, is "the reason—in fact, the only reason—why" Hempel, Levi and Maher "do not end up with Lockeanism" (5). My counterexample needs no such restriction: it keeps the unrestricted option space and every premise Dorst states, and it changes only the value of combinations of beliefs. Holguín (2022, §5) argues that rational thinking is non-monotonic in credence and that sophisticated Lockeanism therefore fails. Theorem 2 shows that accuracy-first epistemology, once it drops Separability, can be non-monotonic too.
+The closest prior work makes the novelty claim narrow. Rothschild (2023) develops additive scoring representations of Lockean belief and explicitly invites investigation of weaker additivity assumptions. Wang (2025, §§2 and 6) studies general expected-utility rules on deductively cogent belief states; his framework permits nonadditive utility and derives convexity constraints. Non-Lockean expected-utility belief is therefore already established. The present contribution sought is an unrestricted-option counterexample directed at Dorst's §4.2, together with a characterization of one holistic score. It does not obtain its result by excluding inconsistent or nonclosed states. Holguín (2022, §5) independently argues that rational thinking need not be monotone in credence. Here that behavior is obtained within an accuracy-maximizing model.
 
 The stakes extend beyond formal epistemology. If accuracy alone settled the bridge between credence and belief, the Lockean would win that debate by default. If, as I argue, the shape of the bridge depends on how we value combinations of true beliefs, then the debate over Lockean, closed and guess-like belief becomes a debate about epistemic value, and accuracy-first epistemology is a framework for conducting it rather than a verdict.
 
