-**Theorem 4.** No such expected-value rule satisfies S together with AS and C for two of the three answers. The premise set is minimal: dropping any one of S, expected-value maximization, truth-directedness, informativeness, AS, C, or the second answer leaves a satisfiable set.
-
-The idea of the proof is this. Let $x^*$ be the credence in $a$ above which $\{a\}$ is always believed; C puts $x^*\ge 1/2$. Split-invariance spreads the verdict along the whole slice $P(a)=x^*$. At the slice's endpoints a two-answer disjunction is certain, so the value of believing $\{a\}$ there is at least $\max(T_{ab},T_{ac})$. Just below $x^*$, where the residual is split evenly, some other answer is chosen, and by continuity it ties with $\{a\}$ at $x^*$. Every candidate leads to a contradiction except the complement $\{b,c\}$, and that candidate forces $T_{bc}>\max(T_{ab},T_{ac})$. Running the same argument for $b$ forces $T_{ac}>T_{bc}$. The independence models are in the Appendix and were machine-checked. The probabilist normality rule satisfies S, AS and C for every threshold $t\in(1/2,1)$, so it is not an expected-value rule. The record measure, by contrast, is split-sensitive: at $(0.75,0.125,0.125)$ its strongest belief is $\{w_1\}$; at $(0.75,0.01,0.24)$ it is $\{w_1,w_3\}$.
-
-Two calibrations. First, Theorem 4 concerns values assigned to answers, with a world-independent value for each false answer. Restricted to closed states, the record measure is of this form, because a closed state's false members at a world $w$ outside its core are equal in number for every such $w$. The general state-level case is open. I conjecture that it also excludes split-invariance. Second, the theorem costs knowledge-aimed theorists little if they never sought an accuracy grounding. Goodman and Salow follow "the tradition in epistemology of thinking of belief as aiming at knowledge" while agreeing that "'believe' in English expresses something considerably weaker" (2023, 113 n. 21). What the theorem blocks is a hybrid: closed, knowledge-aimed belief with an accuracy-first foundation. It also differs in form from Wang's (2025) reported result that the stability theory of Leitgeb (2014) violates the norms implied by expected-utility binarization. Theorem 4 isolates a single checkable property, and it covers the normality rule. I have not been able to check whether the normality rule violates Wang's convexity norms.
-
-## 7. Objections
-
-*The premium is not accuracy; it double-counts the conjunction.* The premium is alethic, since it depends only on which believed propositions are true. It is not a disguised belief in the core: in the four-world case it accrues on $\{w_1\}$, which is not believed. And it respects strict truth-directedness, the only constraint Dorst places on holistic measures. The objector may insist that genuine accuracy is local, so that a belief's contribution depends only on its own truth. That insistence is ME, and by Theorem 1 it is Separability waiting to happen. To exclude the premium on those grounds is to deny the holism that §4.2 set out to accommodate. The reply leaves one thing open: someone may find the premium unmotivated as a value. Then the dispute is about the value of an error-free record. That is my thesis.
-
-*Dorst meant only belief-dependence: values that vary with what else is believed, not with whether it is true.* The text resists this reading: the step "assumes only that $A_{B_0}$ is well-defined." Grant the reading anyway. ME then becomes an explicit premise, and the content gap remains. Under ME, Theorem 1(a) leaves a world-independent $\kappa$. If $\kappa$ is non-additive, Proposition 5 fails; if it is additive, the measure is separable. On the charitable reading, then, Proposition 5 still does not follow from the stated premises, and the holism it accommodates is empty.
-
-*Sequence-Irrelevance should be read strongly, as requiring background-independent thresholds.* So read, it is no longer about sequences. It is BI under another name, and Dorst's defence of it (renumbering the questions on an exam should not change the rational answers, 16) supports only the order reading. The strong reading also undercuts §4.2's conclusion. Showing that a holist must accept BI is just showing that the holist must accept Separability at the margin.
+*Sequence-Irrelevance should be read strongly, as requiring background-independent thresholds.* This is a substantive alternative reading, but it goes beyond invariance under reordering an exam. Background-independent thresholds need not be identified with background-independent marginal values. Theorem 1 concerns the latter, stronger condition. A defender who imposes threshold independence directly owes an argument for why an accuracy holist must accept it; appealing only to order independence leaves the issue unsettled.
 
 *Maximize Expected Accuracy gives only a necessary condition; perhaps neither optimum is rational.* Then rationality would be unsatisfiable at $C$ and $C'$. Accuracy First would then have yielded a norm that no state can meet, which no accuracy-firster accepts. Permission is the minimal premise needed for the framework to issue verdicts at all.
 
+*The premium is representation-dependent.* The value of a record may change when the proposition space is refined: the additive part counts more propositions, while the premium is paid once. This is a serious objection to treating the measure as a complete account of epistemic value. The counterexample fixes a finite proposition space, the same unrestricted space used in the target derivation, and compares two credences on it without changing the questions or their values. Its nonmonotonicity therefore does not arise from switching representations between the two cases. But nothing proved here establishes invariance across different descriptions of the same inquiry. A positive theory based on record value would need to say which questions count and how their weights survive refinement. The limited negative result does not depend on having solved that further problem: the proposed derivation must either exclude the score by a stated premise or accommodate its verdicts. Calling that exclusion a representation-invariance requirement would make the substantive restriction visible.
+
 *The result is negative only; Dorst's picture survives.* In part, yes. Belief remains a function of credence and contextual values, and Dorst's "best guess" gloss fits core-relative Lockeanism at least as well as Lockeanism. What does not survive is the claim that accuracy forces the Lockean form. That matters to Holguín's argument, which targets the Lockean form. Non-monotonicity tells against sophisticated Lockeanism, but not against accuracy-first epistemology as such.
 
-## 8. Conclusion
+## 7. Conclusion
 
-I have shown three things. Dorst's derivation of Lockeanism without Separability is sound exactly for separable measures (Theorem 1). A natural holistic measure satisfying all his stated premises violates Proposition 5 for every positive premium (Theorem 2). That measure delivers core-relative Lockeanism, under which belief is consistent, filtered and non-monotone (Theorem 3). On the boundary, no truth-directed answer-level value yields split-invariant belief (Theorem 4). I have not shown that Lockeanism is false or that the premium is the right value. Nor have I settled the state-level case of Theorem 4 or whether any accuracy measure reproduces Holguín's horse race. What follows is a reallocation of the burden. The accuracy-first argument for the Lockean thesis is exactly as strong as the case for Separability. The choice between thresholds and best hypotheses is a choice about whether a flawless body of belief is worth more than its parts.
+The record measure gives a precise obstruction to the claimed inevitability of Lockean belief. It evaluates every belief state, rewards true additions, penalizes false additions, and judges final states without regard to formation order. With the explicit permission bridge, it nevertheless licenses a nonmonotone relationship between credence and belief for every positive premium. Its optimum has an exact core-and-threshold description; consistency and closure require the additional conditions stated in Theorem 3.
+
+The diagnosis is deliberately limited. ME plus BI restores separability on the specified domain, but separability has not been proved necessary for every Lockean output. Nor has the record premium been established as the uniquely correct measure of accuracy. What changes is the burden of argument: a defence of Lockeanism must justify a restriction that excludes this kind of holistic value, rather than taking order-independent accuracy maximization to have excluded it already.
 
