-*Proof of Theorem 4.* Fix $a$ and let $x^*=\inf\{x:\{a\}$ is chosen at every $P$ with $P(a)>x\}$. AS gives $x^*<1$; C gives $x^*\ge1/2$. For $y>x^*$, $\{a\}$ is optimal on the whole slice $P(a)=y$. By continuity, its value $V=x^*T_a+(1-x^*)F_a$ is maximal on the slice $P(a)=x^*$; at the slice's endpoints this gives $V\ge T_{ab}$ and $V\ge T_{ac}$. There are $y_n\to x^*$ with $y_n\le x^*$ such that $\{a\}$ is not chosen at the even point $E_{y_n}=(y_n,\frac{1-y_n}{2},\frac{1-y_n}{2})$: for $y_n>1/2$ this follows by S from a non-$\{a\}$ point on the slice, and for $y_n<1/2$ by C. Some answer $G\ne\{a\}$ is chosen infinitely often, and its value at $E_{x^*}$ equals $V$. Now $G=W$ gives $T_W=V\ge T_{ab}>T_W$. $G=\{a,b\}$ gives the value $\frac{1+x^*}{2}T_{ab}+\frac{1-x^*}{2}F_{ab}<T_{ab}\le V$; $\{a,c\}$ is symmetric. $G=\{b\}$ has a strictly larger value at the endpoint $(x^*,1-x^*,0)$ than at $E_{x^*}$, so it would beat $\{a\}$ there; $\{c\}$ is symmetric. Hence $G=\{b,c\}$, whose value is constant on the slice, and $T_{bc}>(1-x^*)T_{bc}+x^*F_{bc}=V\ge\max(T_{ab},T_{ac})$. The same argument for $b$ yields $T_{ac}>T_{bc}$, a contradiction. Independence models: drop S, with size-only values $T=(1,0.6,0.5)$ for one-, two- and three-answer $G$, and $F=0$; drop expected-value maximization, with the normality rule, $t\in(1/2,1)$; drop AS, with $T=(0.1,1,0.9)$, $F=0$; drop C, with $T=(1,0.5,0.4)$, $F=0$; drop truth-directedness, with $T_{\{i\}}=3$, $F_{\{i\}}=-2$, $T_{G}=F_{G}=1$ for two-answer $G$, $T_W=0.5$; drop informativeness, with $T_{\{i\}}=1$, $F_{\{i\}}=-1$, $T_G=0$, $F_G=-1$ for two-answer $G$, $T_W=0.2$; keep AS and C for one answer only, with $(T,F)=(1,-1)$ for $\{a\}$, $(1,-10)$ for $\{b\},\{c\}$, $(0.7,-10)$ for $\{a,b\},\{a,c\}$, $(8,0)$ for $\{b,c\}$, $T_W=0.5$. Each model was verified on a grid of mesh $1/240$ to satisfy every remaining premise. ∎
